{"name":"Sea Rover","hex":"#a3d1e2","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"sea rover","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A herring.","One who travels the ocean; one who spends a great deal of time at sea."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sea_rover"}],"entries":[{"title":"Sea Rover","blocks":[{"text":"Sea Rover may refer to:"},{"items":["HMS Sea Rover (P218), a British Royal Navy submarine commissioned in 1943 and sold in 1949","USS Sea Rover (AT-57), originally SP-1014, a United States Navy tugboat in commission from 1918 to 1921"]}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea_Rover"},{"title":"Rover (The Prisoner)","blocks":[{"text":"Rover is a plot device from the 1967 British television programme The Prisoner, and was a crucial tool used to keep 'prisoners' from escaping the Village. It was depicted as a floating white balloon that could coerce, and, if necessary, incapacitate or kill recalcitrant inhabitants of the Village. It also had the ability to subdivide. Several aspects of the Rover device were left unexplained and to the imagination of the viewer."}],"image":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/d/d0/Rover_%28The_Prisoner%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Rover_(The_Prisoner)"},{"title":"Sea","blocks":[{"text":"A sea is a very large body of salty water. The term has two distinct meanings. The first is the interconnected body of seawater that spans most of Earth, known as the sea or the ocean. The second is a named body of water: usually either a marginal sea, or a large, nearly landlocked body of water."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg/330px-Mediterranean_Sea_16.61811E_38.99124N.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sea"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/w